<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bath</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Aquae Sulis, Bath, Somerset</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bath is a city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somerset">Somerset</a>, south-west <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, built around natural hot springs. As Roman Aquae Sulis it had a great bathing complex and a temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulis-minerva">Sulis Minerva</a>. An abbey was founded there in the 7th century, Edgar was crowned there in 973, and its Georgian terraces made it a fashionable spa. It is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bath.jpg" alt="Bath" width="320" height="278" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Diliff · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Roman_Baths_in_Bath_Spa,_England_-_July_2006.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Ceawlin, who ruled in the later sixth century and was listed by Bede among the kings who held authority over the southern English, expanded westwards; the Chronicle records his victory at the Battle of Dyrham in 577, which brought Gloucester, Cirencester and <b>Bath</b> under West Saxon control.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-wessex">Kingdom of Wessex · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Religion mixed Roman and Celtic cults, as at the spring of Sulis Minerva at <b>Bath</b>, where some 130 curse tablets survive.</blockquote><a href="/empire/roman-britain">Roman Britain · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Inscriptions record hundreds of divine names, many of them local: Lugus, remembered in the name of Lugdunum, modern Lyon; Epona, the horse goddess, popular in the Roman army; Cernunnos, named on a carved pillar from Paris; and Sulis, goddess of the hot springs at <b>Bath</b>.</blockquote><a href="/culture/celts">Celts · Beliefs &amp; Worldview</a></li><li><blockquote>The words of &quot;Danny Boy&quot; were written in 1910 in <b>Bath, Somerset</b>, by the English lawyer and lyricist Frederic Weatherly, first to a tune other than the one now sung.</blockquote><a href="/songs/danny-boy">Danny Boy</a></li><li><blockquote>…in the early 17th century: John Playford's collection of dance tunes of 1728 has it as &quot;The New <b>Bath</b>&quot;, the Victorian musicologist William Chappell linked it to a work of 1622, &quot;Sir Edward Nowell's Delight&quot;, and in 1907 the composer Ernest Walker called the melody &quot;three centuries' evolution…</blockquote><a href="/songs/the-british-grenadiers">The British Grenadiers</a></li><li><blockquote>From his garden in <b>Bath</b>, Herschel spotted an object he first took for a comet.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/march-13">13 March in history</a></li></ul></section>
